@using System.Globalization
@inject PreferenceStore Preferences

@* THE ONLY CONTROL A TILE CARRIES, and nearly every line of it is a constraint rather than a taste.

   A <button type="button"> WITH aria-pressed, and not any of the three things it looks like. Not a
   checkbox: a checkbox is for a value submitted with a form, there is no form here, and the control's
   effect is immediate. Not a link: it navigates nowhere. Not a <a role="button">: a real button already
   answers Enter and Space, takes focus in the right order and needs no key handling of its own.

   aria-pressed IS WRITTEN AS A STRING AND NEVER AS A bool. Blazor renders a bool-valued attribute by
   PRESENCE -- true emits `aria-pressed`, false emits NOTHING AT ALL -- and an absent aria-pressed makes
   this an ordinary button that reads "Favourite, Se7en, 1995, button" with no state in it. The unstarred
   state is the one that matters and it is the one that would vanish, so the ternary below is load-bearing
   and FavouriteButtonTests reads both values out of the DOM.

   THE NAME NEVER CHANGES WITH THE STATE. It is "Favourite, Se7en, 1995" whether the film is starred or
   not, because aria-pressed is what says which -- a name that flipped to "Unfavourite" would say the
   state twice, in two different ways, and SC 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value is satisfied by the name plus the
   state and not by the name alone. It is also what makes the control findable by voice ("click
   Favourite") in both states, which SC 2.5.3 Label in Name is about.

   THE TITLE AND THE YEAR ARE IN THE NAME, which is why this takes them as parameters rather than reading
   "Favourite" alone. Sixty of these sit on one page; a wall of sixty controls all named "Favourite" is
   SC 4.1.2 met to the letter and useless in a screen reader's controls list, where the name is all there
   is to choose by. Commas rather than an em dash: a comma is a pause in every screen reader, an em dash
   is read aloud as "em dash" by some and swallowed by others.

   NO aria-label, NO title ATTRIBUTE AND NO id. All three are forbidden here by rules that predate this
   control -- PosterTileTests.TheTileCarriesNoAuthorLabelAndNoTitleAttribute and
   TheTileCreatesNoIdReferenceAndNoIdAtAll, and MarkupRules' id-reference contract -- and all three were
   the obvious way to name a star. The visually-hidden span IS the accessible name, it is real text, and
   it cannot drift out of step with an attribute nobody can see.

   THE ICON IS aria-hidden AND focusable="false". Hiding it is what stops the name being read twice;
   focusable="false" is for Internet Explorer's old SVG focus behaviour and costs nothing to keep. One
   <path> and not two: the filled and the hollow star are the SAME geometry with fill and stroke swapped,
   which the stylesheet does, so there is no way for the two states to drift into different shapes.

   IT ANNOUNCES NOTHING. Pressing it changes one title's own state, the state is on the control the reader
   just pressed, and aria-pressed is how a screen reader reports that -- an announcement would be the same
   fact a second time from a region racing the route announcer. This component takes NO Announcer and
   SourceContractTests.TheTileAndItsControlTakeNoAnnouncer pins that. The one thing it cannot say for
   itself is a browser REFUSING to save, which is not about this title at all, so that goes to the page
   through Refused -- see CouldNotSave. *@
<button type="button" class="tile-fav" aria-pressed="@(marked ? "true" : "false")" @onclick="ToggleAsync">
    <svg class="tile-fav-star" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="22" height="22">
        <path d="M12 17.27 18.18 21l-1.64-7.03L22 9.24l-7.19-.61L12 2 9.19 8.63 2 9.24l5.46 4.73L5.82 21z" />
    </svg>
    <span class="visually-hidden">@Name</span>
</button>

@code {
    /// <summary>
    /// What the page says when the browser refuses to keep the change.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// <b>The sentence lives here and the page renders it,</b> because this control is the thing that
    /// learns the write failed and the page is the only thing with a place to say so. It names the storage
    /// as per-browser in the same breath: a reader who has just been told a change was not saved needs to
    /// know that nothing on a server was lost either, and this app has no server to lose it on. Every
    /// other control that writes the same document -- the rating radios, the list checkboxes -- reports
    /// through this same constant, so a reader meets one sentence for one cause rather than three
    /// paraphrases of it.
    /// </remarks>
    internal const string CouldNotSave =
        "This browser would not save the change. Your favourites and ratings are stored in this browser "
        + "only, and it is full or has storage turned off.";

    /// <summary>What the control is drawing, which is the saved state until a press changes it.</summary>
    private bool marked;

    /// <summary>Which half of the document this title is filed under.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public required TitleKind Kind { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// The published key: <c>K</c> for a film, the slug for a series. Never the TMDB id.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// <c>I</c> is 0 on every unmatched record, so an id-keyed document would file all thirty of them
    /// under one entry and star them together. See <c>RecordKey</c> for why the key is not permanent and
    /// why <see cref="TmdbId"/> is saved beside it.
    /// </remarks>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public required string Key { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// The record's TMDB id, saved beside the key so a re-keyed record can be found again. 0 for an
    /// unmatched record, and 0 is written as 0 -- see <c>PreferenceStore.RecoverMovedKeysAsync</c>.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public int TmdbId { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The title, for the control's name. The visible title on the tile says the same words.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public required string Title { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The year, for the control's name; absent where the record has none.</summary>
    [Parameter] public int? Year { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Called when the browser refused to save. The page renders <see cref="CouldNotSave"/>; this control
    /// has already put itself back to the state the browser actually holds.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public EventCallback Refused { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// The accessible name, and the year is NEVER grouped: 1995, not 1,995. A year is an identifier and
    /// <c>ShowTextTests</c> rules on the same point for a season number. InvariantCulture because this is
    /// four digits with no separator in any culture, so there is nothing for a culture to decide.
    /// </summary>
    internal string Name => Year is { } year
        ? string.Create(CultureInfo.InvariantCulture, $"Favourite, {Title}, {year}")
        : "Favourite, " + Title;

    /// <summary>
    /// Reads the saved state on every parameter set rather than once.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// <b>Because the tile is recycled.</b> <c>PosterGrid</c> keys its items, so paging or filtering hands
    /// THIS component instance a different film, and a state read only in <c>OnInitialized</c> would leave
    /// the previous film's star on the new film's tile. The store is in memory and the read is a dictionary
    /// lookup, so doing it per parameter set costs nothing. It cannot undo an optimistic press either: the
    /// store's own document is already changed by then, and a refused write puts it back (see
    /// <c>PreferenceStore.EditAsync</c>).
    /// </remarks>
    protected override void OnParametersSet() => marked = Preferences.IsFavourite(Kind, Key);

    /// <summary>
    /// Stars or unstars the title, drawing the new state before the write rather than after it.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// <b>Optimistic, and the revert is the half that matters.</b> Waiting for localStorage to answer would
    /// leave the press with no visible effect for a frame on the one platform where the write is slow, so
    /// the star changes first. A browser that then refuses -- a full quota, site data blocked -- would
    /// otherwise leave a star on screen for something nothing remembers, which is the silent loss this
    /// whole path exists to avoid: the state goes back and the page says why.
    /// </remarks>
    private async Task ToggleAsync()
    {
        var next = !marked;
        marked = next;

        try
        {
            await Preferences.SetFavouriteAsync(Kind, Key, TmdbId, next);
        }
        catch (PreferenceStorageException)
        {
            marked = !next;
            await Refused.InvokeAsync();
        }
    }
}
